  "prompt_text": "This figure maps the geography of hunger and instability across Africa by overlaying three related patterns: chronic malnutrition, food shortages, and major famine zones, along with dots marking major conflicts in the 1990s. The broad impression is that food insecurity is concentrated in a belt stretching from the Sahel across central and eastern Africa, with additional hotspots in the Horn of Africa and parts of southern Africa.\n\nLight blue shading marks countries with widespread chronic malnutrition, while dark blue highlights areas of food shortages. Red regions indicate the main famine-prone zones between 1970 and 2000, and these often overlap or sit adjacent to conflict locations. This visual overlap suggests a strong association between famine, persistent undernourishment, and political or armed instability.\n\nParticularly affected areas appear to include the Sahel from Mauritania and Mali through Niger and Chad, the Sudan–Ethiopia–Somalia region, and a large swath of central to southeastern Africa including the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Angola, and Mozambique. In contrast, much of North Africa and parts of southern Africa are left unshaded or less affected. Overall, the figure emphasizes that famine in Africa is not isolated, but linked to wider regional patterns of chronic food stress and conflict.",
